 modes don't really decouple  except that in this small  small motion limit they do  and we can then compute from theory  right because they do a first part of  this experiment they measure the spring  constant  we also ship them small digital scales  those are cheap so they can measure the  masses and so on  and we've talked about normal modes and  oscillations of these sorts of systems  in lecture right so there's a theory  section where they compute these things  you can see that the agreement between  theory and experiment here  is really good  and i mean i'm i'm just  really impressed by the ability of  cell phones and feature tracking to uh  to take  really high quality data so the last  experiment i want to talk about  is waves on an elastic string one of the  things that we've just started in  lecture  we haven't gotten there in lab yet um is  right uh waves in a continuous medium  okay so uh we shipped them  uh some elastic string okay  and here you see my setup um in the  beginning i was really worried about  providing a uniform background  it turns out that feature image feature  track really  is pretty good if the background isn't  uniform but  and so i just took this and i stretch  the string  and i put these uh you know red marks  here with a red marker  um and you can see some and then i took  the string and i pulled it down  okay and let it go now this gets filmed  at  the fastest rate your smartphone will do  which in my case is 248 frames a second  uh and this early motion here image  feature track actually  fails to track because there is still  it's a little hard to see here but  there's still too much motion blur  okay so it loses the  the red dots but if  you wait a little while and just uh trim  your video  until that some of that motion blur  disappears it will  track okay and so here what i'm showing  you um  okay i track a little over 3000 frames  okay uh with uh  you know the ends plus uh nine data  points  uh from these uh red dots so we're  accumulating over 30 000  pieces of data and here i'm showing you  the transverse displacement  at a fixed point on this on the  string one of the red marks that's close  to the center  the insert here shows you this short  time motion  see it's pretty nice and periodic okay  but there are a couple of  and right i can now extract that motion  from all the dots  and plot that as a function of time it  looks like what a damped elastic string  should look like  and then of course i can use some  fourier analysis  to fish out the uh  the string frequencies um  and i've done this two ways i've  extracted a string  the frequency from the early time data  just by fitting it okay and i've  extracted them  with a fourier transform and with the  fourier transform we see actually that  we can see two modes here  the others have decayed away  [Music]  okay but the fundamental mode here  and then at almost exactly twice that  we see small amplitude of a second mode  that got excited when i pulled the  string down  so with a smartphone and  image feature track you can really  extract  very high quality rich data from  quite complicated physical systems and i  actually think that  when we return to you know physical labs  we will want to rethink some of them  and ask you know what can we do using  the ability to track many frames of  video  and high quality video from a smartphone  to do interesting physics experiments  and uh  for those that are interested i've just  listed  um you know what we sent them for these  experiments  we needed to keep it cheap because right  our universities in financial straits  due to covid  but i've left a little time for  questions so  i'd be more than happy to answer  any questions you might have  okay and um i believe uh brandon can  unmute you too if you wish to ask a uh  a question by by talking rather than  typing  okay so any questions at all um we have  one question  in the past we'll check yes  uh  uh i'm bringing that up now so  uh but i don't see uh here we go it's  just a little slow loading  uh  uh oh  uh oh yeah so jose wants uh wants uh  comment on the error analysis  um yeah so um  so our students are terrible at error  analysis  which i think  you know most students are so i thought  one thing that i would take advantage  of here is the advantage to really  do a little more error analysis than  we normally do and  so what i uh  there's a early discussion there's a  separate error analysis notebook  where we make them do the error analysis  really by hand  okay and then we make heavy use  of a round okay to propagate errors  and uh we make you know  use of the uh fit parameters from  fine fit and non-linear fit to get those  errors  and so one of the things i've really  tried to emphasize  this time is if you're going to compare  theory to experiment  you've got to understand your errors  i see some other questions so i have um  okay i don't know if i've really  answered jose's question  satisfactorily but uh the slides have  been uploaded  uh so they should be under files  and then there's a question from  danielle about  the the dot mb files  you know that lie behind this that  really explain  the labs that you didn't see  so i can i'll go ahead and upload those  to the files later this afternoon  and i'll upload not only the dot mb  files  but i'll upload the video and i'll  try to do that with a sensible directory  structure  so that if you wish to import the video  and play around yourself  you can do that  okay we've got a couple more minutes  before we'll  uh get cut off so if there are any other  questions  um so i see that jeff said something  about  um some of these could be adopted for  high school students  um i certainly think the  helmholtz resonator  could be done well and the simple  harmonic motion labs  i think those could be done by high  school students without a doubt  we have done for our first year students  they normally do an air track experiment  with  pasco sensor